---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import MeiosisSim from '../../simulators/meiosis/MeiosisSim.astro';
import { meiosisQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/meiosis';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What meiosis is for' },
  { id: 'numbers', label: 'Chromosomes, homologues and chromatids' },
  { id: 'meiosis1', label: 'Meiosis I' },
  { id: 'meiosis2', label: 'Meiosis II' },
  { id: 'crossing', label: 'Crossing over' },
  { id: 'assortment', label: 'Independent assortment' },
  { id: 'variation', label: 'How much variation?' },
  { id: 'mitosis', label: 'Meiosis vs mitosis' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 11: Meiosis and Sexual Reproduction. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/11-introduction' },
  { text: 'Alberts, B., et al. (2022). Molecular Biology of the Cell (7th ed.), Chapter 17: Meiosis. W. W. Norton.' },
  { text: 'Page, S. L., & Hawley, R. S. (2003). Chromosome choreography: the meiotic ballet. Science, 301(5634), 785–789.' },
  { text: 'Hassold, T., & Hunt, P. (2001). To err (meiotically) is human: the genesis of human aneuploidy. Nature Reviews Genetics, 2(4), 280–291.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="meiosis"
  description="Interactive meiosis simulator: watch homologous chromosomes pair, cross over and separate through meiosis I and II, then flip their arrangement and make gametes to see how genetic variation arises."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={meiosisQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      You share half your DNA with each parent, yet you are not identical to your brothers or sisters. The reason is
      meiosis, the special cell division that makes sperm and eggs. Follow homologous chromosomes as they pair, swap pieces
      and separate, then shuffle them yourself to see how much variety meiosis can create.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <MeiosisSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Meiosis step by step:</strong> press <strong>Start</strong>, or use <strong>Previous stage</strong> and <strong>Next stage</strong>. Drag <strong>Position in meiosis</strong> to examine each separation slowly.</li>
      <li>Click any chromosome to see whether it came from the mother or the father, and whether it has crossed over.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>New random meiosis</strong> to change the metaphase I arrangement and crossover points, and compare the four cells at the end with the starting cell.</li>
      <li><strong>Genetic variation lab:</strong> click each pair to flip its orientation and watch the gametes change. Press <strong>Make 4 gametes</strong> repeatedly and count how many different types appear, with crossing over on and off.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What meiosis is for</h2>
  <p>
    In sexual reproduction, two <strong>gametes</strong> (a sperm and an egg in animals) fuse at fertilisation. If each
    carried a full set of chromosomes, the number would double every generation. <strong>Meiosis</strong> prevents this by
    halving the chromosome number, making <strong>haploid</strong> gametes (n) from a <strong>diploid</strong> cell (2n).
    Fertilisation then restores the diploid number: n + n = 2n. Meiosis also shuffles the parents’ genes, so every gamete
    is genetically unique.
  </p>

  <h2 id="numbers">Chromosomes, homologues and chromatids</h2>
  <p>
    A diploid cell has two of each kind of chromosome: <strong>homologous chromosomes</strong>, one inherited from each
    parent. Homologues have the same size and carry the same genes, but may carry different versions of them
    (<strong>alleles</strong>). Before meiosis the DNA is copied, so each chromosome consists of two identical
    <strong>sister chromatids</strong>. Keep these apart: homologues come from different parents; sister chromatids are
    copies of each other. Humans have 46 chromosomes in 23 pairs, and gametes have 23.
  </p>

  <h2 id="meiosis1">Meiosis I</h2>
  <p>
    In <strong>prophase I</strong>, chromosomes condense and homologues pair up along their length
    (<strong>synapsis</strong>), forming a bivalent of four chromatids, and crossing over takes place. In
    <strong>metaphase I</strong> the pairs line up across the middle of the cell. In <strong>anaphase I</strong> the
    homologues separate to opposite poles while sister chromatids stay joined. After <strong>telophase I</strong> and
    cytokinesis there are two haploid cells, each with one chromosome from every pair. Because the chromosome number is
    halved, meiosis I is called the <strong>reduction division</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="meiosis2">Meiosis II</h2>
  <p>
    Meiosis II follows without another round of DNA replication and works like mitosis. In each cell the chromosomes line
    up singly in <strong>metaphase II</strong>, the sister chromatids separate in <strong>anaphase II</strong>, and after
    <strong>telophase II</strong> and cytokinesis there are four haploid cells, each with one chromatid of every
    chromosome.
  </p>

  <h2 id="crossing">Crossing over</h2>
  <p>
    While homologues are paired, non-sister chromatids can break and rejoin at the same point, exchanging matching
    segments. The crossing points are visible as <strong>chiasmata</strong>. The result is <strong>recombinant</strong>
    chromatids carrying a new mix of the mother’s and father’s alleles. Human chromosomes usually have one to three
    crossovers each, at positions that vary from cell to cell.
  </p>

  <h2 id="assortment">Independent assortment</h2>
  <p>
    At metaphase I, each homologous pair lines up in a random orientation, independently of every other pair. The maternal
    chromosome of pair 1 might face the same pole as the paternal chromosome of pair 2, or not. Each gamete therefore
    receives a random mixture of maternal and paternal chromosomes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="variation">How much variation?</h2>
  <p>
    With n pairs, independent assortment alone gives 2ⁿ possible gametes. For humans that is 2²³, about
    <strong>8.4 million</strong>. Crossing over multiplies this enormously, and <strong>random fertilisation</strong>, any
    sperm meeting any egg, squares it again: about 70 trillion combinations from one couple even without crossing over.
    This variation is the raw material for natural selection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mitosis">Meiosis vs mitosis</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Mitosis</th><th>Meiosis</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Divisions</td><td>One</td><td>Two</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Homologues pair and cross over</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, in prophase I</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What separates</td><td>Sister chromatids</td><td>Homologues (I), then sister chromatids (II)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Result</td><td>2 identical diploid cells</td><td>4 different haploid cells</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Haploid (n) / diploid (2n)</dt>
    <dd>One set of chromosomes / two sets, one from each parent.</dd>
    <dt>Bivalent (tetrad)</dt>
    <dd>A pair of homologous chromosomes joined during prophase I, made of four chromatids.</dd>
    <dt>Chiasma</dt>
    <dd>The point where non-sister chromatids have crossed over.</dd>
    <dt>Non-disjunction</dt>
    <dd>Failure of chromosomes to separate properly, giving gametes with an extra or missing chromosome; it causes conditions such as Down syndrome.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Homologous chromosomes are identical.”</strong> They carry the same genes but often different alleles.</li>
    <li><strong>“DNA is copied again before meiosis II.”</strong> It is copied only once, before meiosis I.</li>
    <li><strong>“Each gamete gets all of one parent’s chromosomes.”</strong> Independent assortment mixes them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Meiosis makes four haploid, genetically different cells from one diploid cell.</li>
    <li>Meiosis I separates homologues (reduction); meiosis II separates sister chromatids.</li>
    <li>Crossing over and independent assortment create new combinations of alleles.</li>
    <li>Fertilisation restores the diploid number and adds still more variation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Model note: the simulator uses 2 or 3 chromosome pairs with one crossover per pair between two adjacent non-sister
    chromatids (the lab picks any non-sister pair). Real cells have more chromosomes, can have several crossovers per pair,
    and in many species meiosis is unequal: in human females only one of the four cells becomes an egg.
  </p>
</SimulatorLayout>
